      "text": "To solve the problem, we start with the given polynomial equation and the information about its roots. The polynomial is \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) and we know that two of its roots are \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\). Let's denote the third root by \\(r\\). Therefore, the polynomial can be expressed as:\n\n\\[a(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r) = 0.\\]\n\nFirst, we expand the product \\((x - 3)(x + 2)\\):\n\n\\[(x - 3)(x + 2) = x^2 - x - 6.\\]\n\nNext, we multiply this result by \\((x - r)\\):\n\n\\[(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r.\\]\n\nThus, the polynomial can be written as:\n\n\\[a(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r) = ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar.\\]\n\nBy comparing the coefficients of the expanded polynomial with the original polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we get:\n\n\\[b = -a(r+1),\\]\n\\[c = a(r-6),\\]\n\\[d = 6ar.\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\). Substituting the expressions for \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) into \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a(r+1) + a(r-6)}{a} = \\frac{-a(r+1) + ar - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-ar - a + ar - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-a - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\) is \\(\\boxed{-7}\\).",
