      "text": "Let's solve the given problem step by step.\n\nWe are given that the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + d = 0\\) has roots \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\). We need to find the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\).\n\nFirst, we use the fact that if \\(r_1\\) and \\(r_2\\) are the roots of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + d = 0\\), then the quadratic equation can be written as:\n\\[a(x - r_1)(x - r_2) = 0.\\]\n\nHere, the roots are \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), so the quadratic equation is:\n\\[a(x - 3)(x + 2) = 0.\\]\n\nExpanding this, we get:\n\\[a(x^2 - 3x + 2x - 6) = a(x^2 - x - 6) = ax^2 - ax - 6a.\\]\n\nThus, the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + d = 0\\) can be written as:\n\\[ax^2 - ax - 6a = 0.\\]\n\nFrom this, we can identify the coefficients:\n\\[b = -a \\quad \\text{and} \\quad d = -6a.\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\). Substituting the values of \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) (which is the coefficient of \\(x\\)), we get:\n\\[b = -a \\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = -a.\\]\n\nSo,\n\\[\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a + (-a)}{a} = \\frac{-2a}{a} = -2.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value is:\n\\[\\boxed{-2}.\\]",
